<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ingush people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ingush are a Nakh people of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a>, closely related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechens">Chechens</a>, living mainly in the Republic of Ingushetia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Organised in clans in mountain villages with stone towers, they adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> largely in the 18th and 19th centuries. In 1944 Stalin deported them with the Chechens to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, and their return in 1957 left a territorial dispute with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-ossetia-alania">North Ossetia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ingush-people.jpg" alt="Ingush people" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ingush_Highlanders.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Ingush call themselves Ghalghai, a name most often linked to the word ghāla, tower or fortress, so that it means people of towers. Georgian sources knew them as Gligvi and Dzurdzuks, and Russian sources as Kists. Their ethnonym Ingush comes from the medieval village of Angusht, which by the end of the 17th century was a large settlement in the Tarskoye Valley.</p><p>In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> the territory of Ingushetia lay where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maykop">Maykop</a> and Kuro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Araxes</a> cultures met, and the Koban culture, dated from the 12th to the 4th century BCE, is usually tied to the origins of the proto-Ingush. Late in the 1st millennium BCE Koban tribes formed a large union known as Malkh or, in Georgian sources, Dzurdzuketi, which controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darial-pass">Darial Gorge</a>. It was defeated at the beginning of the 2nd century BCE by the Seleucid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the late 9th to the early 13th century the Ingush belonged to the kingdom of the Alans, and some Ingush researchers place its capital, Magas, on the territory of present-day Ingushetia. On 4 to 6 March 1770, 24 Ingush elders swore an oath of allegiance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> at Barta-Bos, near Angusht, although each side treated such oaths as a union treaty and relations stayed much the same.</p><p>In the 19th century the Ingush resisted Russian expansion in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasian-war">Caucasian War</a> and were considered half-conquered. In 1860 their lands formed the Ingushskiy Okrug of the Terek Oblast, and the 1897 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 47,409 Ingush in the empire.</p><p>In the Soviet period an Ingush alphabet based on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> script was introduced in 1923 and the first newspaper in the language, Serdalo, appeared that year. The 1939 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 92,120 Ingush. They were exiled en masse in 1944. In 1992 the Republic of Ingushetia was formed within the Russian Federation, and armed clashes with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ossetians">Ossetians</a> over the Prigorodny district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-ossetia-alania">North Ossetia</a> killed 583 people according to the Russian prosecutor's office. The capital Magas was founded in 1995.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Ingush are predominantly Sunni Muslims and speak the Ingush language. Their society was traditionally classless, built on clans and unwritten law, with about 350 clans in Ingushetia today. Villages were governed by senior family members and acted together only in great danger, and the Ingush had no titles giving civil authority over others. Their stone architecture is tied to life in the mountains: a family had a tower, a church and a necropolis. Combat towers usually had five or six levels and a stepped pyramidal roof, with the entrance on the second floor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ingush people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the February Revolution of 1917, a congress at Vladikavkaz founded the Union of United Mountaineers of the North Caucasus and Dagestan, bringing together Chechens, <b>Ingush</b>, Ossetians, Kabardians, Dagestanis and others.</blockquote><a href="/state/mountainous-republic-of-the-northern-caucasus">Mountainous Republic of the Northern Caucasus</a></li><li><blockquote>Its princes extended their power over neighbouring Ossetians, <b>Ingush</b>, Balkars and Abazins.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-kabarda">Principality of Kabarda</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1920 the Russian SFSR crushed them, abolished the host and deported many stanitsas, giving their lands to Chechens and <b>Ingush</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/terek-cossack-host">Terek Cossack Host</a></li><li><blockquote>It covered the lands of the Terek Cossack Host and of the Chechens, <b>Ingush</b>, Ossetians and Kabardians, with its government at Pyatigorsk and Vladikavkaz.</blockquote><a href="/state/terek-soviet-republic">Terek Soviet Republic</a></li></ul></section>
